---
import PieFigure from "./PieFigure.astro";
---

<section class="om-reference" aria-label="Feed rate, loading rate and mass">
  <div class="om-block">
    <h2>The middle slot decides</h2>
    <div class="om-table-wrap">
      <table class="om-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">On your sheet</th>
            <th scope="col">What you have</th>
            <th scope="col">What you get</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="om-row-name">Feed Rate</span>
              <span class="om-row-sub">You are adding a chemical</span>
            </td>
            <td>a <strong>dose</strong> and a <strong>flow</strong> in MGD</td>
            <td class="om-row-out">pounds per day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="om-row-name">Loading Rate</span>
              <span class="om-row-sub">Something is coming at you</span>
            </td>
            <td>a <strong>concentration</strong> and a <strong>flow</strong> in MGD</td>
            <td class="om-row-out">pounds per day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="om-row-name">Mass</span>
              <span class="om-row-sub">Something is sitting in a tank</span>
            </td>
            <td>a <strong>concentration</strong> and a <strong>volume</strong> in MG</td>
            <td class="om-row-out is-total">pounds, total</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="om-note is-check">
      <span class="om-note-label">THE TELL</span>
      <p>
        Look at the middle number. A <strong>flow</strong> moving past you gives
        a rate &mdash; pounds <strong>per day</strong>. A <strong>tank volume</strong> sitting still gives a total &mdash; just <strong>pounds</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="om-block">
    <h2>On shift</h2>

    <div class="om-split">
      <div class="om-panel-card">
        <span class="om-badge" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
        <h3>Feed rate &mdash; you are adding it</h3>
        <div class="om-scenario">
          <span class="om-scenario-label">AT THE PANEL</span>
          <p>
            You are carrying <strong>2.0 mg/L</strong> of chlorine into <strong>0.5 MGD</strong>. How much chlorine leaves the day tank?
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="2.0 times 8.34 times 0.5 equals 8.3 pounds per day">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>2.0</strong><span>DOSE<br />MG/L</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card is-conversion"><strong>8.34</strong><span>LB PER<br />GALLON</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card"><strong>0.5</strong><span>FLOW<br />MGD</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>8.3</strong><span>LB/DAY</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>
          That is the number you order against and the number your day tank has
          to keep up with.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="om-panel-card">
        <span class="om-badge is-blue" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
        <h3>Loading rate &mdash; it is coming at you</h3>
        <div class="om-scenario">
          <span class="om-scenario-label">FROM THE LAB SHEET</span>
          <p>
            Influent BOD came back at <strong>180 mg/L</strong> and the plant is
            taking <strong>2.0 MGD</strong>. How much BOD is the plant actually
            being asked to treat?
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="180 times 8.34 times 2.0 equals 3002 pounds per day">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>180</strong><span>BOD<br />MG/L</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card is-conversion"><strong>8.34</strong><span>LB PER<br />GALLON</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card"><strong>2.0</strong><span>FLOW<br />MGD</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>3,000</strong><span>LB/DAY BOD</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Same arithmetic as the feed rate. Nobody dosed anything &mdash; this is
          what showed up at the headworks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="om-panel-card">
      <span class="om-badge" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
      <h3>Mass &mdash; it is sitting in the tank</h3>
      <div class="om-split" style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        <div>
          <div class="om-scenario">
            <span class="om-scenario-label">WALKING THE AERATION BASIN</span>
            <p>
              Your basin holds <strong>0.75 MG</strong> and the mixed liquor is
              running <strong>2,400 mg/L</strong>. How many pounds of solids are
              under you right now?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="om-equation" aria-label="2400 times 8.34 times 0.75 equals 15000 pounds">
            <div class="om-card"><strong>2,400</strong><span>MLSS<br />MG/L</span></div>
            <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
            <div class="om-card is-conversion"><strong>8.34</strong><span>LB PER<br />GALLON</span></div>
            <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
            <div class="om-card"><strong>0.75</strong><span>VOLUME<br />MG</span></div>
            <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
            <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>15,000</strong><span>LB SOLIDS</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            No <em>per day</em> on the end. This is inventory &mdash; the pounds
            you are holding. It is the starting point for wasting decisions and
            for sludge age.
          </p>
        </div>
        <PieFigure highlight="feed" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="om-note">
    <p>
      <strong>Why 8.34?</strong> It is the weight of one gallon of water in
      pounds. It is what lets you turn a concentration in mg/L into pounds,
      which is the only unit a pump, a scale or a purchase order understands.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
